{"question_id": "V2-dev-BOOK-en-T3-002", "approach": "MQ_6000", "approach_base": "MQ_6000", "flags": {}, "setting": "en-en", "q_lang": "en", "d_lang": "en", "corpus": "K", "corpus_set": "BOOK_en", "pool_id": "BOOK-en", "answer_text": "Frederick Douglass - model: Webster's Spelling Book Italics and Master Thomas's copy-books (also letters learned from ship-yard carpenters marking timber)\nFrederick Douglass - practice: wrote with a lump of chalk on the board fence, brick wall, and pavement, and copied into the spaces left in Master Thomas's copy-books until his hand was very similar to Master Thomas's\nBenjamin Franklin - model: his own arguments in writing copied fair and exchanged in letters with a friend, with his father's critique of his manner of writing\nBenjamin Franklin - practice: did writing exercises and read at night after work or before it began in the morning, or on Sundays when alone in the printing-house\n\nDouglass practised by copying existing written models (spelling-book Italics and Thomas's copy-books) using chalk on fences, walls, and pavement, while Franklin practised by composing and copying out his own arguments in letter exchanges and refining his style after his father's criticism, working alone at night or on Sundays in the printing-house.", "abstained": false, "evidence_items": [{"doc_id": "K.gutenberg-23_douglass_narrative.en", "section_id": "K.gutenberg-23_douglass_narrative.en::o012", "chars": 968, "unit_ids": ["K.gutenberg-23_douglass_narrative.en::p1_b120"], "cut": false}, {"doc_id": "K.gutenberg-23_douglass_narrative.en", "section_id": "K.gutenberg-23_douglass_narrative.en::o012", "chars": 508, "unit_ids": ["K.gutenberg-23_douglass_narrative.en::p1_b120"], "cut": false}, {"doc_id": "K.gutenberg-23_douglass_narrative.en", "section_id": "K.gutenberg-23_douglass_narrative.en::o012", "chars": 957, "unit_ids": ["K.gutenberg-23_douglass_narrative.en::p1_b119", "K.gutenberg-23_douglass_narrative.en::p1_b120"], "cut": false}, {"doc_id": "K.